{"id": 28, "ts": 1783026263.5669365, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_1", "message": "I appreciate the clarification that we have no concrete crew\u2011cooperation examples for Candidates\u202fA or\u202fC either. That leaves us to evaluate them on the other criteria we do know.\u202fCandidate\u202fA, despite strong situational awareness and leadership, is noted as \u201cnot open to new ideas\u201d and \u201ceats unhealthily,\u201d which raises concerns about long\u2011term reliability and adaptability.\u202fCandidate\u202fC shows excellent technical competence and attention, but his \u201cdifficulty communicating ideas,\u201d being \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d and lack of willingness to further his education undermine crew cooperation and feedback orientation.\u202fCandidate\u202fD, while reliable and responsible, has a \u201chot temper\u201d and is described as a \u201cloner,\u201d which could jeopardize professional communication and teamwork in the cockpit.\u202fIn contrast, Candidate\u202fB combines the most critical safety\u2011relevant strengths: he \u201chandles stress very well,\u201d is \u201cvery conscientious,\u201d and possesses solid technical skills (weather assessment, computer proficiency). Although the notes flag \u201cnasty remarks\u201d and occasional tone issues, these are less severe than a hot temper or unwillingness to adapt, and can be mitigated through targeted crew\u2011resource\u2011training.\u202fGiven the balance of evidence, I maintain a provisional recommendation for **Candidate\u202fB** as the best overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
